There is no application code in this directory (it holds only Claude config), so this is a standalone TypeScript version that wraps whatever function fetches a profile today; `fetchProfile` and the 60-second TTL are my assumptions.

```ts
type Profile = unknown; // replace with your profile type

const TTL_MS = 60_000;
const cache = new Map<string, { value: Promise<Profile>; expires: number }>();

export function getProfile(
  userId: string,
  fetchProfile: (id: string) => Promise<Profile>,
): Promise<Profile> {
  const hit = cache.get(userId);
  if (hit && hit.expires > Date.now()) return hit.value;

  const value = fetchProfile(userId);
  cache.set(userId, { value, expires: Date.now() + TTL_MS });
  // Do not keep failed lookups around for the whole TTL.
  value.catch(() => {
    if (cache.get(userId)?.value === value) cache.delete(userId);
  });
  return value;
}

export function invalidateProfile(userId: string): void {
  cache.delete(userId);
}
```

Call `invalidateProfile(userId)` in every code path that writes a profile, otherwise users see their old data for up to a minute after saving.

It caches the promise, so concurrent requests for the same user share one upstream call.

I left out a size cap and a shared store: entries are only replaced on re-fetch, never evicted, so add an LRU limit if the user set is large, and move to Redis once you run more than one instance, since each process caches and invalidates separately.

